Here are the rules governing peacefulness in 3.4.3:

"Normal" monster creation:

Some monsters are always created peaceful.  These include
    the Quest Leader and Quest Guardians (except for randomly
    created ninjas, who seem to be created hostile), shopkeepers,
    guards, prisoners, the Oracle, aligned priests, watchmen,
    watch captains, Charon, co-aligned unicorns, and the mail demon.
Most monsters are always created hostile.  These include everything
    EXCEPT: acid blobs, little dogs, dogs, large dogs, kittens,
    housecats, large cats, gremlins, hobbits, dwarves, bugbears,
    dwarf lords, dwarf kings, homunculi, imps, quasits, tengus,
    goblins, hobgoblins, orcs, hill orcs, Mordor orcs, Uruk-hais,
    orc shamans, orc-captains, white/gray/black unicorns, ponies,
    horses, warhorses, couatls, Aleaxi, Angels, ki-rins, Archons,
    bats (but all giant/vampire bats are hostile), plains/forest/
    mountain centaurs, air/fire/earth/water elementals, gnomes,
    gnome lords, gnomish wizards, gnome kings, giants, stone/hill/
    fire/frost/storm giants, TITANS, red/black/golden/guardian
    nagas and hatchlings, ogres, ogre lords, ogre kings, garter
    snakes, umber hulks, monkeys, apes, sasquatches, humans, elves,
    woodland/green/grey elves, elf-lords, Elvenkings, the monsters
    listed above as always created peaceful, high priests, Charon,
    djinnis, sandestins, randomly created non-player role characters.
Most other monsters are either "peace minded" or not.  This
    depends on whether there are good or bad race relations as follows:
    humans hate gnomes and orcs, elves like elves but hate orcs,
    dwarves like dwarves and gnomes but hate orcs, gnomes like
    dwarves and gnomes but hate humans, orcs hate humans, elves,
    and dwarves.  If race is not an issue, then monsters with
    different alignment are hostile.  Chaotic monsters will be
    hostile to players with the amulet.  Minions are hostile if
    you have strayed.  Otherwise, there is a random chance that
    the monster will be peace minded based on how strongly aligned
    you are, and how strongly aligned the monster is.

Exceptions:

If the room is a court (throneroom), the monsters are never peaceful.
Your pet is always created tame.
Groups of monsters are not created all peaceful (this is to
    avoid blocking you in).
End game role characters are created hostile.

Monsters created in other ways:

If the monster was a tame monster from a bones file, make it not
    tame and not peaceful.
Monsters created from blessed figurines are tame 80%, untame 10% and
    peaceful 10%. Monsters created from uncursed figurines are tame
    10%, untame 80% and peaceful 10%.  Monsters created from cursed
    figurines are tame 10%, untame 10% and peaceful 80%. Same with
    djinnis created (freed) by rubbing B/U/C magic lamps.
When you quaff a smokey potion and free a djinni, the djinni
    will be tame 5% if the potion is blessed, 20% if the potion
    is uncursed, and 5% if the potion is cursed.  It will be
    peaceful the same proportion of times, and will be hostile
    5%, 20% and 80% of the time if the potion is B/U/C.  The
    rest of the time, the djinni vanishes (after sometimes giving
    you a wish).
When a monster quaffs a smokey potion and frees a djinni, the djinni
    will be peaceful (half the time, the djinni immediately
    vanishes).
Zapping your dead pet with a wand of undead turnings will sometimes
    result in a live, tame pet.
If you are a demon, and are not a foocubus or a balrog, you can
    summon tame demon pets.
If you are a werecritter, you can summon tame critters.
Killer bees that are created when you kick a tree are always
    non-peaceful.
Monster summoned insects or snakes are created hostile.
Monster summoned nasty creatures are created hostile.
Minions summoned by an angry god are created hostile.
Demons summoned through chaotic sacrifice of the same race
    are created peaceful.
If Schroedinger's cat is alive, it will be peaceful.
Chopping a peaceful worm in two will reate a new worm that
    has a chance of being peaceful based on your luck.
Some ways to tame a monster:

You can throw gold at a robbed shopkeeper to placate him.
Throwing gold at a priest causes him to thank you, but doesn't
    do anything ele.
You can throw gold at a mercenary (Soldier, Sergeant, Lieutenant,
    Captain) to tame him (2/3 chance if you pay enough).  The exact
    amount required is based on how much gold is in your inventory,
    the type of mercenary (Captains need more than Lieutenants, etc.),
    your current level, and your charisma (I didn't know that!)
Throwing sufficient gold or paying an angry shopkeeper will pacify
    him.
Throwing gems at a unicorn pacifies it (and will also affect your
    luck, of course).
Rodney, Medusa, and the Quest Nemeses can never be made tame.
    Other monsters can sometimes be made tame with music, potions,
    reading scrolls of taming, spells of charm monster, untrapping,
    feeding it treats, or reading the blessed book of the dead
    around undead monsters.  Too many details for here.
Demon princes can be bribed with a big enough fee.  Note: if you
    are wielding Excalibur, demon princes really don't like that.
    Also, if you have the amulet, it won't work.
Chaotic priests/knights have a chance of chasting turn undead
    against an undead monster and making that monster peaceful.

Things that make monsters mad:

If the monster is a watch captain in town and can see you dig through a
    closed door, a wall, a fountain or a tree, with a wand or spell of
    digging, or you have been previously warned about digging before,
    the monster will become angry.
Kicking doors/robbing stores in town also angers the guards.
Robbing shops angers shopkeepers.
Making a fountain in town go away by quaffing/dipping in it makes
    guards angry.
Successfully dipping for Excalibur in town always angers the guards,
    even if the fountain doesn't dry up.
Picking a locked door in town when a guard can see will get you
    a warning for a first offense.  If you've been warned already,
    he gets angry.
Freeing prisoners on the Quest makes the prisoners and your god
    happy, but angers the guards.
Your pet becomes less tame the longer you abandon him.  If you
    abandon dim too long (if his tameness is greater than the
    number of moves you've been separated divided by 150, rounded
    up) then he will either become untame (tameness/wildness chance)
    or unpeaceful (the rest of the time).
Tame, non-minion pets that eat meat or vegetables become nonpeaceful
    if they go too long without eating (500 turns after getting
    hungry for monsters with less than 3 hit points, 750 turns after
    getting hungry otherwise).
If you attempt to free a pet who is caught in a trap by moving
    into it, there is a 1 in tameness chance that you will
    cause it to me unpeaceful.
If you move into a peaceful, undetected, ceiling hidden monster,
    and the monster is not a piercer, then he always becomes
    unpeaceful.
Attacking them.  Attacking your Quest Leader will also anger
    his or her guardians.
Not following a vault guard will eventually make him angry.



